1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:01,939
Dendroarchaeology.

2
00:00:02,395 --> 00:00:06,961
This explanation supports a ninety-minute dendroarchaeology lesson.

3
00:00:07,421 --> 00:00:11,832
Pause to complete the dating and interpretation exercises.

4
00:00:12,635 --> 00:00:16,281
Explain the questions addressed by dendroarchaeology.

5
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:20,453
Distinguish outer-ring, felling and construction dates.

6
00:00:20,914 --> 00:00:25,190
Use preservation and context to state conclusions and limits.

7
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:30,769
The aim is to explain the evidence linking a numerical date to an event, not merely to

8
00:00:30,782 --> 00:00:32,620
report a year.

9
00:00:33,423 --> 00:00:37,167
Crossdating assigns calendar years to preserved rings.

10
00:00:37,623 --> 00:00:41,531
The last measured ring may not be the original outermost ring.

11
00:00:41,991 --> 00:00:45,853
Now interpret the dates in their archaeological context.

12
00:00:46,307 --> 00:00:51,688
Even with secure measurement and crossdating, interpreting construction or repair requires

13
00:00:51,702 --> 00:00:55,832
the object's structural and archaeological context.

14
00:00:56,635 --> 00:00:58,235
Ring formation.

15
00:00:58,690 --> 00:00:59,762
Felling.

16
00:01:00,216 --> 00:01:02,249
Working / storage.

17
00:01:02,703 --> 00:01:04,782
Construction / use.

18
00:01:05,243 --> 00:01:08,243
Repair / reuse / deposition.

19
00:01:08,703 --> 00:01:11,618
Intervals can separate the stages shown.

20
00:01:12,071 --> 00:01:17,868
Before equating felling with use, ask what evidence constrains the time between them.

21
00:01:18,671 --> 00:01:22,731
Bark edge survives: approach the final growth increment.

22
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:27,680
Some sapwood survives: missing outer rings may be estimated.

23
00:01:28,134 --> 00:01:32,535
Heartwood only: a precise felling date is harder to establish.

24
00:01:32,989 --> 00:01:38,081
The meaning of the last preserved ring depends on whether bark, sapwood or only inner wood

25
00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:39,784
survives.

26
00:01:40,587 --> 00:01:43,963
Inspect the outermost increment beneath the bark.

27
00:01:44,418 --> 00:01:48,366
Check incomplete growth and possible cutting after tree death.

28
00:01:48,826 --> 00:01:52,852
Relate final growth to felling only with supporting conditions.

29
00:01:53,313 --> 00:01:58,681
A complete outer edge tightly constrains felling, but intra-ring position and dormancy can

30
00:01:58,695 --> 00:02:01,484
still matter to the event interval.

31
00:02:02,287 --> 00:02:05,295
Identify the heartwood–sapwood boundary.

32
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:09,776
Use a sapwood model appropriate to species and population.

33
00:02:10,237 --> 00:02:14,263
Do not transfer another region’s ring-count range uncritically.

34
00:02:14,723 --> 00:02:20,513
Estimating missing sapwood requires appropriate species and regional information, rather

35
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:23,688
than adding a universal number of rings.

36
00:02:24,491 --> 00:02:28,722
A terminus post quem is a lower bound, not an upper bound.

37
00:02:29,177 --> 00:02:34,099
A last surviving ring of 1580 excludes felling before 1580.

38
00:02:34,559 --> 00:02:38,085
Unknown missing rings leave the upper bound unresolved.

39
00:02:38,546 --> 00:02:42,289
A lower bound indicates that the wood formed through that year.

40
00:02:42,743 --> 00:02:46,972
The amount of missing outer growth remains a separate issue.

41
00:02:47,775 --> 00:02:50,611
Examine earlywood and latewood development.

42
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:54,863
Growth timing varies with species, site and climate.

43
00:02:55,317 --> 00:02:58,659
A winter interval may span two calendar years.

44
00:02:59,119 --> 00:03:04,067
Felling-season inference examines the developmental state of outer cells and requires

45
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:07,736
evidence about preservation and the growth season.
